{"id": "eng-0001", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 1, "text_native": "Hello", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "say", "why": "The default greeting anywhere. Works with every age and every status.", "exchange_id": null, "exchange_turn": null, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0002", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 1, "text_native": "Hi", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "informal", "direction": "say", "why": "Informal hello. Fine with peers and anyone younger. Slightly too familiar for an official or an older stranger.", "exchange_id": null, "exchange_turn": null, "caution": "Using this to someone noticeably older reads as presumptuous. 'Hello' costs nothing and is always safe.", "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0003", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 1, "text_native": "Good morning", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "say", "why": "Correct and slightly warmer than plain 'Hello' before noon. Carries no risk anywhere.", "exchange_id": null, "exchange_turn": null, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0004", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 1, "text_native": "Good afternoon", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "say", "why": "Correct between noon and roughly five. Nobody will notice if you get the boundary wrong, which is the point.", "exchange_id": null, "exchange_turn": null, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0005", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 1, "text_native": "Good evening", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "say", "why": "Correct after about five, and in the evening when arriving anywhere.", "exchange_id": null, "exchange_turn": null, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0006", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 1, "text_native": "Good day", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "say", "why": "How you greet a shopkeeper or a member of staff. Slightly formal, and it signals you are a customer rather than a browser.", "exchange_id": "eng-x0002", "exchange_turn": 1, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0007", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 1, "text_native": "How are you?", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "say", "why": "Not a question. It is the standard opener in a short encounter and everyone knows the expected answer.", "exchange_id": "eng-x0001", "exchange_turn": 1, "caution": "Asking a shopkeeper mid-transaction sounds like you are stalling.", "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0008", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 1, "text_native": "I'm well, thank you. And you?", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "say", "why": "The expected reply, returned back. Short answers without this read as cold or unfriendly.", "exchange_id": "eng-x0001", "exchange_turn": 2, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0009", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 1, "text_native": "Nice to meet you", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "say", "why": "Closes an introduction. Using it when you have already met someone reads as a small mistake, so only say it once.", "exchange_id": "eng-x0009", "exchange_turn": 3, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0010", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 1, "text_native": "My name is", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "say", "why": "The introduction half of meeting anyone. Incomplete on its own — always attach a name.", "exchange_id": "eng-x0009", "exchange_turn": 1, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0011", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 1, "text_native": "What's your name?", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "say", "why": "The other half of an introduction. Costs nothing and is often answered even when the rest of the language is not understood.", "exchange_id": "eng-x0009", "exchange_turn": 2, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0012", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 1, "text_native": "Where are you from?", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "say", "why": "The safest opener in any country. Rarely requires more than one word in return, and people volunteer far more than you asked for.", "exchange_id": "eng-x0001", "exchange_turn": 3, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0013", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 1, "text_native": "I'm from India", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "say", "why": "Answers the most common question you will be asked. The country alone is enough; nobody needs the city.", "exchange_id": "eng-x0009", "exchange_turn": 4, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0014", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 1, "text_native": "I live in", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "say", "why": "Where you are based, as distinct from where you are from. Locals distinguish these and will notice the difference.", "exchange_id": null, "exchange_turn": null, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0015", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 1, "text_native": "This is my first time here", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "say", "why": "Disarms impatience and invites help. Locals relax noticeably when they know you are not a resident being difficult.", "exchange_id": "eng-x0011", "exchange_turn": 2, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0016", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 1, "text_native": "Sorry, my English isn't very good", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "understand", "why": "In every other language in this catalogue this slot holds 'I'm learning your language' and is tagged say. Here it flips to understand: this is what people say TO you, not what you say.", "exchange_id": "eng-x0012", "exchange_turn": 1, "caution": "In English, telling a native speaker their own language is not good is self-deprecating and slightly odd. The slot exists because the structure requires it, not because it is useful speech.", "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0017", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 1, "text_native": "Thank you", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "say", "why": "The single highest-frequency courtesy phrase in English. Underused.", "exchange_id": "eng-x0004", "exchange_turn": 2, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0018", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 1, "text_native": "Thank you very much", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "say", "why": "Stronger than plain 'thank you' and equally cheap. Use it when something genuinely helped you.", "exchange_id": null, "exchange_turn": null, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0019", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 1, "text_native": "Please", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "say", "why": "Goes at the end of a request in English, unlike most of the catalogue where it goes at the start. Position is the whole trick.", "exchange_id": "eng-x0003", "exchange_turn": 1, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0020", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 1, "text_native": "You're welcome", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "say", "why": "Closes a thank-you. Most people do not notice if it is missing, which is why it is near the bottom of this tier.", "exchange_id": "eng-x0004", "exchange_turn": 3, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0021", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 1, "text_native": "Sorry", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "say", "why": "Small apology — brushing past, arriving late, interrupting yourself. The everyday one.", "exchange_id": "eng-x0005", "exchange_turn": 1, "caution": "In British English 'sorry' does the work of both 'sorry' and 'excuse me'. In American English they are more distinct and 'sorry' can read as a bigger admission than you meant.", "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0022", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 1, "text_native": "Excuse me", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "say", "why": "Getting someone's attention politely, or getting past. Softer than 'sorry' in American English.", "exchange_id": "eng-x0006", "exchange_turn": 1, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0023", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 1, "text_native": "I'm sorry", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "say", "why": "The fuller apology, for something that actually happened. Distinct from plain 'sorry' in American English.", "exchange_id": null, "exchange_turn": null, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0024", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 1, "text_native": "That's fine", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "understand", "why": "What people reply to an apology. Included so you recognise it and stop apologising again — over-apologising is the more common mistake.", "exchange_id": "eng-x0005", "exchange_turn": 2, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0025", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 1, "text_native": "No problem", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "informal", "direction": "understand", "why": "The other standard reply to an apology. Slightly less formal than 'that's fine'.", "exchange_id": null, "exchange_turn": null, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0026", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 1, "text_native": "Pardon?", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "say", "why": "British and Australian English for asking someone to repeat. American speakers use 'excuse me?' or say nothing at all.", "exchange_id": "eng-x0008", "exchange_turn": 2, "caution": "In American English this is not understood as a request to repeat, and can sound like an accusation.", "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0027", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 1, "text_native": "Of course", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "understand", "why": "The usual answer when someone asks permission for something small. You will hear it constantly and it means yes.", "exchange_id": null, "exchange_turn": null, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0028", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 1, "text_native": "Welcome", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "understand", "why": "The standard reply to a greeting in some regions. Nothing to say back.", "exchange_id": null, "exchange_turn": null, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0029", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 1, "text_native": "After you", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "say", "why": "Small courtesy at a doorway or in a queue. Costs one word and prevents a physical awkwardness.", "exchange_id": "eng-x0006", "exchange_turn": 2, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0030", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 1, "text_native": "One moment, please", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "say", "why": "Buys you a few seconds. The polite way to make someone wait without appearing to expect it.", "exchange_id": "eng-x0003", "exchange_turn": 2, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0031", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 1, "text_native": "Sorry to bother you", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "polite", "direction": "say", "why": "Softens an interruption. Works on anyone, including officials, where plain 'excuse me' can read as impatient.", "exchange_id": "eng-x0003", "exchange_turn": 1, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0032", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 1, "text_native": "Goodbye", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "say", "why": "Neutral farewell. Works with anyone.", "exchange_id": "eng-x0010", "exchange_turn": 4, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0033", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 1, "text_native": "Have a good day", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "say", "why": "The standard warm farewell to a stranger or a service worker. More natural than bare 'goodbye' with someone you just met.", "exchange_id": "eng-x0002", "exchange_turn": 3, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0034", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 1, "text_native": "Take care", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "say", "why": "Warmer than goodbye and slightly more familiar. Fine with anyone you have just been polite to.", "exchange_id": "eng-x0010", "exchange_turn": 4, "caution": "A little too familiar for a formal or first contact. 'Goodbye' is the safe choice there.", "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0035", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 12, "text_native": "I don't understand", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "say", "why": "The most useful repair phrase in the language. Gives you a way out that does not end the interaction.", "exchange_id": "eng-x0007", "exchange_turn": 2, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0036", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 12, "text_native": "Sorry, could you say that again?", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "say", "why": "Asking for a repeat without sounding demanding. The leading 'sorry' is what makes it polite rather than abrupt.", "exchange_id": "eng-x0008", "exchange_turn": 2, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0037", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 12, "text_native": "Could you speak more slowly, please?", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "say", "why": "The single most effective repair phrase in any language. Asking for slower speech rather than a repeat gives the speaker something to actually do.", "exchange_id": "eng-x0007", "exchange_turn": 3, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0038", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 12, "text_native": "I can't hear you", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "say", "why": "For a loud room or a quiet speaker. Distinct from not understanding, and often confused for it.", "exchange_id": null, "exchange_turn": null, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0039", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 12, "text_native": "Do you speak English?", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "understand", "why": "What a local will ask you. Included so you can recognise it and answer without panic.", "exchange_id": "eng-x0012", "exchange_turn": 2, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0040", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 12, "text_native": "A little English", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "understand", "why": "The expected answer to the previous item. Short, and it invites them to switch to simpler speech.", "exchange_id": "eng-x0012", "exchange_turn": 3, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0041", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 12, "text_native": "How do you say this in English?", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "say", "why": "Point at the thing you need. Works at a market, on a menu, on a form. Tier 0 rather than Tier 1 because it needs no vocabulary at all, only a finger.", "exchange_id": null, "exchange_turn": null, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0042", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 12, "text_native": "What does that mean?", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "say", "why": "Points at any word or sign you have just been shown. The same trick as the previous item, aimed at the written form instead of the spoken one.", "exchange_id": null, "exchange_turn": null, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0043", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 12, "text_native": "Can you help me, please?", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "say", "why": "The generic opener for anything you cannot phrase yet. Works when you have no words for the problem — which is most of the time, early on.", "exchange_id": "eng-x0011", "exchange_turn": 1, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0044", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 12, "text_native": "Is that all?", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "say", "why": "How you end an interaction at a counter. Note the rising intonation — flat, it sounds like a statement and the conversation stalls.", "exchange_id": "eng-x0004", "exchange_turn": 1, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0045", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 12, "text_native": "Could you help me with this?", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "neutral", "direction": "say", "why": "Points at a form, a device, a bag, a door handle. Works when you understand the situation perfectly and simply lack the noun for the object.", "exchange_id": null, "exchange_turn": null, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
{"id": "eng-0046", "lang": "eng", "tier": 0, "domain": 12, "text_native": "Just a second", "text_romanized": null, "text_english": null, "register": "informal", "direction": "say", "why": "Informal version of 'one moment'. Cheaper and more natural with people you have already spoken to once.", "exchange_id": null, "exchange_turn": null, "caution": null, "source": {"class": "authored", "id": "LangKraft editorial", "url": null, "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "external_id": null}, "failure_flags": []}
